examples.content:
A program is written to calculate a student's overall grade from three test scores, each expected to be out of 100. The original analysis identified the inputs as "three scores" and the processing as "average the three scores and convert to a grade", but never asked what should happen if a student has been absent for one test and so has no score for it.

During testing, a tester enters a missing score as 0 for the absent test, and the program averages it in as if it were a genuine mark of zero, producing a grade far lower than the student's actual performance on the two tests they did sit.

-- Tracing the fault back --
// Symptom noticed at: TESTING (the wrong grade is produced for this test case)
// Could the CODE be patched directly? Only by adding a special case for a score
//   of 0, which would then wrongly treat a genuine mark of zero the same way.
// Is this really a CODING fault? No: the code correctly implements the design
//   it was given; the design never described how to handle a missing score
//   at all, so there is nothing wrong with how the design was translated into code.
// Is this really a DESIGN fault? No: the design correctly implements the
//   analysis it was given, an average of three scores; a missing score was
//   never part of what the design was asked to handle in the first place.
// Root cause: ANALYSIS never identified "a test may have no score, because the
//   student was absent" as a case the program needed to handle at all.

-- Correct fix --
// Return to ANALYSIS: identify that a missing score is a distinct case, decide
//   how it should be represented (e.g. a special value, not 0) and how it should
//   affect the average (e.g. average only the scores that exist).
// Only then revise the DESIGN's algorithm to handle this case, and only then
//   update the CODE to match the revised design.
// Patching only the code at the point testing found the fault would leave the
//   design and the analysis both still silently missing the same case.
